// ChaCha20, Poly1305, and the AEAD_CHACHA20_POLY1305 construction that
// composes them -- RFC 8439 (which obsoletes RFC 7539 without changing the
// bits). One module, not three, because RFC 8439 itself is: the two
// primitives exist in this codebase for exactly one reason each other needs,
// the one-time Poly1305 key comes out of the ChaCha20 block function, and a
// caller of an AEAD cipher never wants the pieces separately (`docs/http.m31`
// and `json.m31` are this codebase's own precedent for one module covering a
// standard that is naturally one thing).
//
// It exists for `docs/ssh-decision.md` Phase 1: `chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com`
// is the one cipher suite that library will ever negotiate, and RFC 8439's
// AEAD construction is exactly what that OpenSSH cipher runs per packet.
// Standalone and not wired into anything yet -- see that decision doc.
//
// API
// ---
//   chacha20poly1305.block(key, counter, nonce)       -> bytes, 64 octets
//   chacha20poly1305.openssh_block(key, counter, nonce) -> bytes, 64 octets
//   chacha20poly1305.encrypt(key, counter, nonce, pt) -> bytes, len(pt)
//   chacha20poly1305.key_gen(key, nonce)              -> bytes, 32 octets
//   chacha20poly1305.mac(key, msg)                    -> bytes, 16 octets
//   chacha20poly1305.seal(key, nonce, plaintext, aad) -> bytes, ciphertext || tag
//   chacha20poly1305.open(key, nonce, sealed, aad)     -> Result<bytes, Error>
//
// --- `openssh_block`: a second word layout, for `docs/ssh-decision.md` Phase 2 --
//
// `block` is RFC 8439's IETF layout: a 32-bit counter in word 12, a 96-bit
// nonce in words 13-15. `chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com`
// (`/usr/share/doc/openssh/PROTOCOL.chacha20poly1305`) is built on the
// older, original Bernstein layout instead: a 64-bit counter split across
// words 12-13, and an 8-byte nonce (there, the SSH packet sequence number)
// in words 14-15. These are not interchangeable and one is not a special
// case of the other -- word 13 means "high 32 bits of the counter" in one
// and "part of the nonce" in the other, so padding the 8-byte OpenSSH nonce
// out to 12 bytes and calling `block` would compile, run, and produce a
// keystream that is wrong but plausible-looking. `openssh_block` exists so
// that mistake has nowhere to happen: it is `block`'s same permutation,
// with only the last four words' assignment changed to match.
// `key` is always 32 octets and `nonce` always 12 -- the one modern IETF
// variant, which is what `chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com` and every other
// current use of this pair means by "ChaCha20". A wrong length is a bug in
// the caller, not the world, so it **traps** (reference §6.6) rather than
// returning a `Result` -- the same choice `Array`'s fixed length and
// `bytes.push`'s range make. What a `Result` *is* for here: `open`'s tag
// check, because a forged or corrupted ciphertext is exactly the world a
// network parser has to expect, the same reasoning `base64.decode` gives for
// its own `Error`.
//
// --- 32-bit arithmetic on a 64-bit int -------------------------------------
//
// ChaCha20 words are kept non-negative and below 2^32, exactly as
// `apps/git/sha1.m31` and `lib/crypto/sha256.m31` keep theirs, for the same
// reasons: `>>` is then already a logical shift, `+` on a handful of such
// words never approaches the 64-bit trap threshold, and `& 0xFFFF_FFFF`
// spells "modulo 2^32" at the one place each addition or rotate needs it.
//
// --- Poly1305: radix 2^26, not a bignum library ----------------------------
//
// RFC 8439 §3 warns against a generic big-number library for this: dynamic
// allocation, and a variable-time codepath, in the one place where the
//'variable' is the reader's own authentication key. This is Andrew Moon's
// public-domain `poly1305-donna` 32-bit construction (§2.5's accumulator held
// as five 26-bit limbs rather than one 130-bit number), the reference nearly
// every constant-time, allocation-free Poly1305 in production is some
// translation of, chosen for exactly the property RFC 8439 asks for: fixed,
// input-independent work, no allocation, and -- because every intermediate
// product is a 26-bit limb times at most a 29-bit one, summed five at a time
// -- nothing within a factor of 60 of this language's 2^63 overflow trap
// even at the most adversarial input (verified in the differential fuzz
// harness, `tests/ssh/t_chacha20poly1305.m31`, which is also checked against
// RFC 8439 Appendix A.3's eleven vectors built specifically to stress the
// reduction: a zero `r`, a carry out of the top limb, an accumulator landing
// on exactly 2^130-5, and the two "5*H+L" 131-bit-intermediate cases).
//
// Poly1305 is one-shot rather than a streaming `Hasher` like `sha256`'s: RFC
// 8439's own key generation and the AEAD construction both need the whole
// padded buffer built before a tag can be computed, so there is no caller
// this codebase has that would use a streaming update.
//
// --- constant time where it has to be --------------------------------------
//
// `open`'s tag comparison is `consttime.is_equal` (`lib/crypto/consttime.m31`), not
// `==`: `bytes`'s `==` (reference §3.10) makes no promise about its timing, and
// a MAC check whose time leaks *where* the first mismatched byte was is a
// forgery oracle -- the exact class of bug RFC 8439 §3 is warning about one
// paragraph over from the big-number one.

import crypto.consttime;

// --- speed notes (docs/CRYPTO-PERF.md) ----------------------------------------
//
// The emitted C pays for three things per operation: a call to the noinline
// `rt_stack_check` at every function entry, a bounds check on every element
// access, and an overflow check on every `+`, `-` and `*`. So the hot paths
// here are shaped for the C compiler rather than for the RFC's pseudocode:
//
//   - the ChaCha20 permutation is ONE function holding all sixteen words in
//     locals (no per-quarter-round call, no `Array` traffic inside the rounds);
//   - words are masked to 32 bits at the top of every double round, which is
//     what lets the C compiler see that `x >> 16` needs no sign fill and that
//     `a + b` cannot overflow, so the checks fold away;
//   - the keystream is XORed straight into a pre-sized output buffer, one
//     whole 64-octet block at a time, not pushed octet by octet through a
//     temporary `bytes`;
//   - Poly1305 runs over the caller's buffer in place (no padded copy of
//     AAD || ciphertext || lengths), keeping its five limbs in locals across
//     all blocks of a segment.
//
// The Poly1305 limb products use `wrapping_mul`/`wrapping_add`: the bound in
// the Poly1305 section below shows they never reach 2^63, so the values are
// identical and the per-operation overflow checks (about 40% of the time) go.
//
// None of this adds a secret-dependent branch or index: every loop bound is a
// public length, there is no table, and the only operations on key-derived
// words are `+`, `^`, shifts, `&`, `|` and `*`, as before.

// --- ChaCha20 ---------------------------------------------------------------

// "expand 32-byte k", as four little-endian 32-bit words -- RFC 8439 §2.3.
const Array<int> CHACHA_CONST = [0x6170_7865, 0x3320_646E, 0x7962_2D32, 0x6B20_6574];

// Four octets of `data` starting at `offset`, little-endian -- the order every
// ChaCha20 and Poly1305 word in this file is read in. Never masked with
// `& 0xFFFF_FFFF`: each term is already narrower than where the next one
// starts (a byte, shifted by at most 24), so the OR can never carry into a
// bit the next term also sets.
int word_le(bytes data, int offset) {
    return data[offset] | (data[offset + 1] << 8) | (data[offset + 2] << 16) | (data[offset + 3] << 24);
}

// The twenty-round permutation and the final "add the original input words"
// step, RFC 8439 §2.3, shared by both word layouts: `state` holds the sixteen
// input words (each below 2^32); the sixteen output words go to `out`.
// All sixteen words live in locals; each of the ten loop iterations is one
// double round (four column quarter rounds, then four diagonal ones), written
// out flat so there is no call and no array access between the loads and the
// stores. RFC 8439 §2.1's quarter round, with a, b, c, d the four indices.
void chacha20_rounds(Array<int> state, Array<int> out) {
    int x0 = state[0];
    int x1 = state[1];
    int x2 = state[2];
    int x3 = state[3];
    int x4 = state[4];
    int x5 = state[5];
    int x6 = state[6];
    int x7 = state[7];
    int x8 = state[8];
    int x9 = state[9];
    int x10 = state[10];
    int x11 = state[11];
    int x12 = state[12];
    int x13 = state[13];
    int x14 = state[14];
    int x15 = state[15];
    for (int round in 0 .. 10) {
        // The words are below 2^32 here (the loop-carried values were masked
        // at the end of the last iteration; the first iteration's come from
        // 32-bit words). Saying so again costs one AND each and is what lets
        // the C compiler drop the sign fill from every `>>` below.
        x0 = x0 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x1 = x1 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x2 = x2 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x3 = x3 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x4 = x4 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x5 = x5 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x6 = x6 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x7 = x7 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x8 = x8 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x9 = x9 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x10 = x10 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x11 = x11 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x12 = x12 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x13 = x13 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x14 = x14 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x15 = x15 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x0 = x0 + x4 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x12 = x12 ^ x0;
        x12 = (x12 << 16 | x12 >> 16) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x8 = x8 + x12 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x4 = x4 ^ x8;
        x4 = (x4 << 12 | x4 >> 20) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x0 = x0 + x4 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x12 = x12 ^ x0;
        x12 = (x12 << 8 | x12 >> 24) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x8 = x8 + x12 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x4 = x4 ^ x8;
        x4 = (x4 << 7 | x4 >> 25) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x1 = x1 + x5 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x13 = x13 ^ x1;
        x13 = (x13 << 16 | x13 >> 16) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x9 = x9 + x13 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x5 = x5 ^ x9;
        x5 = (x5 << 12 | x5 >> 20) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x1 = x1 + x5 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x13 = x13 ^ x1;
        x13 = (x13 << 8 | x13 >> 24) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x9 = x9 + x13 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x5 = x5 ^ x9;
        x5 = (x5 << 7 | x5 >> 25) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x2 = x2 + x6 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x14 = x14 ^ x2;
        x14 = (x14 << 16 | x14 >> 16) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x10 = x10 + x14 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x6 = x6 ^ x10;
        x6 = (x6 << 12 | x6 >> 20) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x2 = x2 + x6 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x14 = x14 ^ x2;
        x14 = (x14 << 8 | x14 >> 24) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x10 = x10 + x14 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x6 = x6 ^ x10;
        x6 = (x6 << 7 | x6 >> 25) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x3 = x3 + x7 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x15 = x15 ^ x3;
        x15 = (x15 << 16 | x15 >> 16) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x11 = x11 + x15 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x7 = x7 ^ x11;
        x7 = (x7 << 12 | x7 >> 20) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x3 = x3 + x7 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x15 = x15 ^ x3;
        x15 = (x15 << 8 | x15 >> 24) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x11 = x11 + x15 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x7 = x7 ^ x11;
        x7 = (x7 << 7 | x7 >> 25) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x0 = x0 + x5 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x15 = x15 ^ x0;
        x15 = (x15 << 16 | x15 >> 16) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x10 = x10 + x15 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x5 = x5 ^ x10;
        x5 = (x5 << 12 | x5 >> 20) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x0 = x0 + x5 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x15 = x15 ^ x0;
        x15 = (x15 << 8 | x15 >> 24) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x10 = x10 + x15 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x5 = x5 ^ x10;
        x5 = (x5 << 7 | x5 >> 25) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x1 = x1 + x6 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x12 = x12 ^ x1;
        x12 = (x12 << 16 | x12 >> 16) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x11 = x11 + x12 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x6 = x6 ^ x11;
        x6 = (x6 << 12 | x6 >> 20) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x1 = x1 + x6 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x12 = x12 ^ x1;
        x12 = (x12 << 8 | x12 >> 24) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x11 = x11 + x12 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x6 = x6 ^ x11;
        x6 = (x6 << 7 | x6 >> 25) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x2 = x2 + x7 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x13 = x13 ^ x2;
        x13 = (x13 << 16 | x13 >> 16) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x8 = x8 + x13 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x7 = x7 ^ x8;
        x7 = (x7 << 12 | x7 >> 20) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x2 = x2 + x7 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x13 = x13 ^ x2;
        x13 = (x13 << 8 | x13 >> 24) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x8 = x8 + x13 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x7 = x7 ^ x8;
        x7 = (x7 << 7 | x7 >> 25) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x3 = x3 + x4 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x14 = x14 ^ x3;
        x14 = (x14 << 16 | x14 >> 16) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x9 = x9 + x14 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x4 = x4 ^ x9;
        x4 = (x4 << 12 | x4 >> 20) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x3 = x3 + x4 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x14 = x14 ^ x3;
        x14 = (x14 << 8 | x14 >> 24) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x9 = x9 + x14 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        x4 = x4 ^ x9;
        x4 = (x4 << 7 | x4 >> 25) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    }
    out[0] = x0 + state[0] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    out[1] = x1 + state[1] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    out[2] = x2 + state[2] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    out[3] = x3 + state[3] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    out[4] = x4 + state[4] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    out[5] = x5 + state[5] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    out[6] = x6 + state[6] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    out[7] = x7 + state[7] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    out[8] = x8 + state[8] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    out[9] = x9 + state[9] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    out[10] = x10 + state[10] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    out[11] = x11 + state[11] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    out[12] = x12 + state[12] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    out[13] = x13 + state[13] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    out[14] = x14 + state[14] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    out[15] = x15 + state[15] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
}

// `state`'s first twelve words (the constant and the key) set from `key`.
Array<int> chacha20_init(bytes key, bytes nonce, int nonce_length) {
    if (key.size() != 32) {
        trap("chacha20: key must be 32 bytes");
    }
    if (nonce.size() != nonce_length) {
        trap("chacha20: nonce must be " + nonce_length.to_str() + " bytes");
    }
    Array<int> state = [0; 16];
    state[0] = CHACHA_CONST[0];
    state[1] = CHACHA_CONST[1];
    state[2] = CHACHA_CONST[2];
    state[3] = CHACHA_CONST[3];
    for (int i in 0 .. 8) {
        state[4 + i] = word_le(key, i * 4);
    }
    return state;
}

// The IETF layout's state for `key` and the 12-octet `nonce`; word 12 (the
// counter) is the caller's.
Array<int> ietf_state(bytes key, bytes nonce) {
    Array<int> state = chacha20_init(key, nonce, 12);
    state[13] = word_le(nonce, 0);
    state[14] = word_le(nonce, 4);
    state[15] = word_le(nonce, 8);
    return state;
}

// The original layout's state for `key` and the 8-octet `nonce`; words 12 and
// 13 (the 64-bit counter) are the caller's.
Array<int> openssh_state(bytes key, bytes nonce) {
    Array<int> state = chacha20_init(key, nonce, 8);
    state[14] = word_le(nonce, 0);
    state[15] = word_le(nonce, 4);
    return state;
}

// One 64-octet keystream block as bytes, for the state's current words 12-15.
bytes block_bytes(Array<int> state) {
    Array<int> words = [0; 16];
    chacha20_rounds(state, words);
    bytes out = [0; 64];
    for (int i in 0 .. 16) {
        int word = words[i];
        out[4 * i] = word & 0xFF;
        out[4 * i + 1] = word >> 8 & 0xFF;
        out[4 * i + 2] = word >> 16 & 0xFF;
        out[4 * i + 3] = word >> 24 & 0xFF;
    }
    return out;
}

// `data[0 .. count)` XORed with the keystream of `state`, starting at block
// `counter`, into `out[0 .. count)` (`out` is at least `count` long and may be
// `data` itself). `wide` is 1 for the original layout, whose 64-bit counter
// spans words 12-13, and 0 for the IETF one, whose word 13 is nonce. Whole
// blocks are XORed word by word; only the last partial block goes octet by
// octet.
void chacha20_xor(Array<int> state, int counter, int wide, bytes data, int count, bytes out) {
    Array<int> stream = [0; 16];
    int position = 0;
    int block_index = counter;
    while (position + 64 <= count) {
        state[12] = block_index & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        if (wide == 1) {
            state[13] = block_index >> 32 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        }
        chacha20_rounds(state, stream);
        for (int i in 0 .. 16) {
            int word = stream[i] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
            int at = position + 4 * i;
            out[at] = data[at] ^ (word & 0xFF);
            out[at + 1] = data[at + 1] ^ (word >> 8 & 0xFF);
            out[at + 2] = data[at + 2] ^ (word >> 16 & 0xFF);
            out[at + 3] = data[at + 3] ^ (word >> 24 & 0xFF);
        }
        position = position + 64;
        block_index = block_index + 1;
    }
    if (position < count) {
        state[12] = block_index & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        if (wide == 1) {
            state[13] = block_index >> 32 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
        }
        chacha20_rounds(state, stream);
        int offset = 0;
        while (position < count) {
            int word = stream[offset >> 2] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
            out[position] = data[position] ^ (word >> ((offset & 3) * 8) & 0xFF);
            position = position + 1;
            offset = offset + 1;
        }
    }
}

// How many blocks `byte_count` octets take.
int blocks_for(int byte_count) {
    return (byte_count + 63) / 64;
}

/// The ChaCha20 block function, RFC 8439 §2.3: one 64-octet keystream block
/// for `key` (32 octets), `counter` (0 to 2^32-1) and `nonce` (12 octets).
/// `key` or `nonce` at any other length, or `counter` outside its range, is a
/// bug in the caller and **traps**, per this module's header. This is the
/// IETF word layout: `counter` alone in word 12, `nonce` across words
/// 13-15 -- see `openssh_block` for the other, incompatible layout
/// `chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com` needs instead.
pub bytes block(bytes key, int counter, bytes nonce) {
    if (counter < 0 || counter > 0xFFFF_FFFF) {
        trap("chacha20: counter must fit in 32 bits");
    }
    Array<int> state = ietf_state(key, nonce);
    state[12] = counter;
    return block_bytes(state);
}

/// The ChaCha20 block function, original (pre-IETF) layout: one 64-octet
/// keystream block for `key` (32 octets), a 64-bit `counter` and an 8-octet
/// `nonce`. `counter` is split little-endian across words 12 (low) and 13
/// (high); `nonce` fills words 14-15, little-endian -- see this module's
/// header for why this is a different, not merely differently-sized,
/// construction than `block`'s IETF layout, and why `chacha20-poly1305@
/// openssh.com` (`docs/ssh-decision.md` Phase 2) needs this one instead.
/// `key` or `nonce` at any other length, or a negative `counter`, traps.
pub bytes openssh_block(bytes key, int counter, bytes nonce) {
    if (counter < 0) {
        trap("chacha20: counter must not be negative");
    }
    Array<int> state = openssh_state(key, nonce);
    state[12] = counter & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    state[13] = counter >> 32 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    return block_bytes(state);
}

/// ChaCha20 encryption, RFC 8439 §2.4: `data` XORed with the keystream that
/// `key`, `nonce` and the block counter starting at `counter` produce.
/// Symmetric -- the same call decrypts, since XOR is its own inverse and the
/// keystream does not depend on `data`. Traps like `block` if the counter
/// would pass 2^32 - 1.
pub bytes encrypt(bytes key, int counter, bytes nonce, bytes data) {
    if (counter < 0 || counter + blocks_for(data.size()) - 1 > 0xFFFF_FFFF) {
        trap("chacha20: counter must fit in 32 bits");
    }
    Array<int> state = ietf_state(key, nonce);
    bytes out = [0; data.size()];
    chacha20_xor(state, counter, 0, data, data.size(), out);
    return out;
}

/// The same XOR in the original layout (`openssh_block`'s): `data` with the
/// keystream for `key`, the 8-octet `nonce` and the 64-bit block counter
/// starting at `counter`. What `chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com` encrypts a
/// packet body (counter 1) with, without a call per 64 octets.
pub bytes openssh_encrypt(bytes key, int counter, bytes nonce, bytes data) {
    if (counter < 0) {
        trap("chacha20: counter must not be negative");
    }
    Array<int> state = openssh_state(key, nonce);
    bytes out = [0; data.size()];
    chacha20_xor(state, counter, 1, data, data.size(), out);
    return out;
}

// --- Poly1305 ----------------------------------------------------------------

// The accumulator and key, as one array so a segment's blocks can run in one
// call with everything in locals. Layout: 0-4 the accumulator `h` (five
// 26-bit limbs), 5-9 the clamped `r` (five limbs), 10-13 `5 * r[1..4]`, 14-17
// the pad `s` (four 32-bit words).
//
// Radix 2^26, not a bignum library -- andrew moon's poly1305-donna 32-bit
// construction (§2.5's accumulator as five 26-bit limbs rather than one
// 130-bit number): fixed, input-independent work and no allocation, which is
// what RFC 8439 §3 asks for. Every product below is a limb below 2^28 times
// one below 2^29, summed five at a time: under 2^60, far from the 2^63
// overflow trap even for adversarial input (tests/ssh/t_chacha20poly1305.m31
// checks RFC 8439 Appendix A.3's eleven vectors built to stress the reduction).

// The state for the one-time key whose eight little-endian words are `k0` to
// `k7`: `r` in the first four, clamped and split into limbs in one step (each
// limb a shifted, masked read of the 128-bit number; the mask is also RFC 8439
// §2.5.1's clamp for the bits it keeps), `s` in the last four.
Array<int> poly_start(int k0, int k1, int k2, int k3, int k4, int k5, int k6, int k7) {
    Array<int> p = [0; 18];
    int r1 = (k0 >> 26 | k1 << 6) & 0x03FF_FF03;
    int r2 = (k1 >> 20 | k2 << 12) & 0x03FF_C0FF;
    int r3 = (k2 >> 14 | k3 << 18) & 0x03F0_3FFF;
    int r4 = k3 >> 8 & 0x000F_FFFF;
    p[5] = k0 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    p[6] = r1;
    p[7] = r2;
    p[8] = r3;
    p[9] = r4;
    p[10] = r1 * 5;
    p[11] = r2 * 5;
    p[12] = r3 * 5;
    p[13] = r4 * 5;
    p[14] = k4;
    p[15] = k5;
    p[16] = k6;
    p[17] = k7;
    return p;
}

// `block_count` whole 16-octet blocks of `data` from `start`, folded into the
// accumulator in `p` and reduced back into (nearly) five 26-bit limbs. `hibit`
// is `1 << 24` for a full block (the implicit 2^128 that RFC 8439 §2.5.1's
// pseudocode adds before every block) and `0` for the last, short block of a
// bare `mac`, where the trailing `0x01` the caller already wrote stands for
// it. The accumulator is left slightly above five 26-bit limbs when the final
// carry itself overflows a limb, which `poly_finish`'s own full carry accounts for.
void poly_blocks(Array<int> p, bytes data, int start, int block_count, int hibit) {
    int h0 = p[0];
    int h1 = p[1];
    int h2 = p[2];
    int h3 = p[3];
    int h4 = p[4];
    int r0 = p[5];
    int r1 = p[6];
    int r2 = p[7];
    int r3 = p[8];
    int r4 = p[9];
    int s1 = p[10];
    int s2 = p[11];
    int s3 = p[12];
    int s4 = p[13];
    int offset = start;
    for (int index in 0 .. block_count) {
        int word_0 = data[offset] | (data[offset + 1] << 8) | (data[offset + 2] << 16) | (data[offset + 3] << 24);
        int word_1 = data[offset + 4] | (data[offset + 5] << 8) | (data[offset + 6] << 16) | (data[offset + 7] << 24);
        int word_2 = data[offset + 8] | (data[offset + 9] << 8) | (data[offset + 10] << 16) | (data[offset + 11] << 24);
        int word_3 = data[offset + 12] | (data[offset + 13] << 8) | (data[offset + 14] << 16) | (data[offset + 15] << 24);
        // The block as five overlapping 26-bit windows of the 128-bit
        // little-endian number it is.
        h0 = h0 + (word_0 & 0x03FF_FFFF);
        h1 = h1 + ((word_0 >> 26 | word_1 << 6) & 0x03FF_FFFF);
        h2 = h2 + ((word_1 >> 20 | word_2 << 12) & 0x03FF_FFFF);
        h3 = h3 + ((word_2 >> 14 | word_3 << 18) & 0x03FF_FFFF);
        h4 = h4 + (word_3 >> 8 | hibit);
        // h *= r, mod 2^130 - 5, a limb at a time: d[i] collects every
        // partial product that lands in limb i, the wraparound from limb 5 and
        // up folded back in by using s[k] = 5 * r[k] (2^130 = 5 mod p).
        int d0 = h0.wrapping_mul(r0).wrapping_add(h1.wrapping_mul(s4)).wrapping_add(h2.wrapping_mul(s3)).wrapping_add(h3.wrapping_mul(s2)).wrapping_add(h4.wrapping_mul(s1));
        int d1 = h0.wrapping_mul(r1).wrapping_add(h1.wrapping_mul(r0)).wrapping_add(h2.wrapping_mul(s4)).wrapping_add(h3.wrapping_mul(s3)).wrapping_add(h4.wrapping_mul(s2));
        int d2 = h0.wrapping_mul(r2).wrapping_add(h1.wrapping_mul(r1)).wrapping_add(h2.wrapping_mul(r0)).wrapping_add(h3.wrapping_mul(s4)).wrapping_add(h4.wrapping_mul(s3));
        int d3 = h0.wrapping_mul(r3).wrapping_add(h1.wrapping_mul(r2)).wrapping_add(h2.wrapping_mul(r1)).wrapping_add(h3.wrapping_mul(r0)).wrapping_add(h4.wrapping_mul(s4));
        int d4 = h0.wrapping_mul(r4).wrapping_add(h1.wrapping_mul(r3)).wrapping_add(h2.wrapping_mul(r2)).wrapping_add(h3.wrapping_mul(r1)).wrapping_add(h4.wrapping_mul(r0));
        // Carry radix-2^26, left to right, the last carry folded back into
        // limb 0 times 5 (again 2^130 = 5 mod p).
        int carry = d0 >> 26;
        h0 = d0 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
        d1 = d1 + carry;
        carry = d1 >> 26;
        h1 = d1 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
        d2 = d2 + carry;
        carry = d2 >> 26;
        h2 = d2 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
        d3 = d3 + carry;
        carry = d3 >> 26;
        h3 = d3 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
        d4 = d4 + carry;
        carry = d4 >> 26;
        h4 = d4 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
        h0 = h0 + carry * 5;
        carry = h0 >> 26;
        h0 = h0 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
        h1 = h1 + carry;
        offset = offset + 16;
    }
    p[0] = h0;
    p[1] = h1;
    p[2] = h2;
    p[3] = h3;
    p[4] = h4;
}

// `data[start .. end)` folded in: whole blocks straight from `data`, a last
// partial block copied into a zeroed 16-octet buffer. `terminated` is 1 for a
// bare `mac` (RFC 8439 §2.5.1: a `0x01` octet after the message, no implicit
// high bit) and 0 for the AEAD's segments (§2.8: zero padding to 16, then
// every block, the padded one included, carries the high bit).
void poly_feed(Array<int> p, bytes data, int start, int end, int terminated) {
    int whole = (end - start) / 16;
    poly_blocks(p, data, start, whole, 1 << 24);
    int taken = start + whole * 16;
    if (taken < end) {
        bytes last = [0; 16];
        for (int i in 0 .. end - taken) {
            last[i] = data[taken + i];
        }
        if (terminated == 1) {
            last[end - taken] = 1;
            poly_blocks(p, last, 0, 1, 0);
        } else {
            poly_blocks(p, last, 0, 1, 1 << 24);
        }
    }
}

// The 16-octet tag: the full carry `poly_blocks` leaves undone, the final
// reduction mod 2^130 - 5, then (h + s) mod 2^128.
bytes poly_finish(Array<int> p) {
    int h0 = p[0];
    int h1 = p[1];
    int h2 = p[2];
    int h3 = p[3];
    int h4 = p[4];
    int carry = h1 >> 26;
    h1 = h1 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    h2 = h2 + carry;
    carry = h2 >> 26;
    h2 = h2 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    h3 = h3 + carry;
    carry = h3 >> 26;
    h3 = h3 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    h4 = h4 + carry;
    carry = h4 >> 26;
    h4 = h4 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    h0 = h0 + carry * 5;
    carry = h0 >> 26;
    h0 = h0 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    h1 = h1 + carry;
    // `h` is below 2*p but may still be at or past p (RFC 8439's own "P" =
    // 2^130-5): compute h - p alongside h, and keep whichever of the two is
    // actually less than p. `g4`'s top bit (bit 31 of its 32-bit range) is 1
    // exactly when `h - p` borrowed, i.e. exactly when h was already below p.
    int g0 = h0 + 5;  // RFC 8439 §2.5.1: h - P
    carry = g0 >> 26;
    g0 = g0 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    int g1 = h1 + carry;  // RFC 8439 §2.5.1: h - P
    carry = g1 >> 26;
    g1 = g1 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    int g2 = h2 + carry;  // RFC 8439 §2.5.1: h - P
    carry = g2 >> 26;
    g2 = g2 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    int g3 = h3 + carry;  // RFC 8439 §2.5.1: h - P
    carry = g3 >> 26;
    g3 = g3 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    int g4 = h4 + carry - (1 << 26) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;  // RFC 8439 §2.5.1: h - P
    // `bit - 1` is all-zero bits when `bit` is 1 and all-one bits when `bit`
    // is 0 (two's complement -1), which turns the single bit into the two
    // masks below -- an arithmetic recreation of the branch so both paths
    // take the same time regardless of which one the result needs.
    int mask = (g4 >> 31 & 1) - 1 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    int notmask = ~mask & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    h0 = h0 & notmask | g0 & mask;
    h1 = h1 & notmask | g1 & mask;
    h2 = h2 & notmask | g2 & mask;
    h3 = h3 & notmask | g3 & mask;
    h4 = h4 & notmask | g4 & mask;
    // Five 26-bit limbs back to four 32-bit words, then tag = (h + s) mod
    // 2^128: a 128-bit little-endian add, each carry taken from the previous
    // word's overflow past 32 bits.
    int tag_0 = (h0 | h1 << 26) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    int tag_1 = (h1 >> 6 | h2 << 20) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    int tag_2 = (h2 >> 12 | h3 << 14) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    int tag_3 = (h3 >> 18 | h4 << 8) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    int word_sum = tag_0 + p[14];
    tag_0 = word_sum & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    word_sum = tag_1 + p[15] + (word_sum >> 32);
    tag_1 = word_sum & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    word_sum = tag_2 + p[16] + (word_sum >> 32);
    tag_2 = word_sum & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    word_sum = tag_3 + p[17] + (word_sum >> 32);
    tag_3 = word_sum & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    bytes out = [0; 16];
    for (int i in 0 .. 4) {
        int word = tag_0;
        if (i == 1) {
            word = tag_1;
        } else if (i == 2) {
            word = tag_2;
        } else if (i == 3) {
            word = tag_3;
        }
        out[4 * i] = word & 0xFF;
        out[4 * i + 1] = word >> 8 & 0xFF;
        out[4 * i + 2] = word >> 16 & 0xFF;
        out[4 * i + 3] = word >> 24 & 0xFF;
    }
    return out;
}

/// The Poly1305 tag, RFC 8439 §2.5: 16 octets, for a 32-octet one-time `key`
/// (`r` in its first 16 octets, `s` in its last, clamped and applied exactly
/// as §2.5.1 specifies) and an arbitrary-length `message`. An **empty**
/// message gets no block at all -- RFC 8439 §2.5.1's loop runs `ceil(0 / 16)`
/// times, so the tag is `s` alone.
pub bytes mac(bytes key, bytes message) {
    if (key.size() != 32) {
        trap("poly1305: key must be 32 bytes");
    }
    Array<int> p = poly_start(word_le(key, 0), word_le(key, 4), word_le(key, 8), word_le(key, 12), word_le(key, 16), word_le(key, 20), word_le(key, 24), word_le(key, 28));
    poly_feed(p, message, 0, message.size(), 1);
    return poly_finish(p);
}

// --- the AEAD construction ---------------------------------------------------

/// The one-time Poly1305 key, RFC 8439 §2.6: the first 32 octets of the
/// ChaCha20 keystream for `key` and `nonce` at counter 0.
pub bytes key_gen(bytes key, bytes nonce) {
    return block(key, 0, nonce).substr(0, 32);
}

/// Why `open` refused to decrypt. Payload-free (docs/errors-decision.md §4):
/// there is nothing to add to either reason that the caller does not already
/// have from `sealed` and `aad`.
pub enum Error {
    /// `sealed` is shorter than a tag, so there is nothing to check.
    Short;
    /// The computed tag does not match the one `sealed` carried: `sealed`,
    /// `aad`, `key` or `nonce` is not what it was sealed with.
    Tag;
}

pub str Error.to_str() {
    match (this) {
        case Short: {
            return "chacha20poly1305: shorter than a tag";
        }
        case Tag: {
            return "chacha20poly1305: authentication tag mismatch";
        }
    }
}

// The Poly1305 state for the AEAD over `aad` and `ciphertext[0 .. ciphertext_length)`:
// one-time key from block 0 of `state`, then RFC 8439 §2.8's `mac_data` fed
// segment by segment (`aad`, padded to 16; the ciphertext, padded to 16; the
// two lengths, 8 octets each) instead of being copied into one buffer first.
Array<int> aead_state(Array<int> state, bytes aad, bytes ciphertext, int ciphertext_length) {
    Array<int> words = [0; 16];
    state[12] = 0;
    chacha20_rounds(state, words);
    Array<int> p = poly_start(words[0], words[1], words[2], words[3], words[4], words[5], words[6], words[7]);
    poly_feed(p, aad, 0, aad.size(), 0);
    poly_feed(p, ciphertext, 0, ciphertext_length, 0);
    bytes lengths = [0; 16];
    int aad_length = aad.size();
    for (int i in 0 .. 8) {
        lengths[i] = aad_length >> (8 * i) & 0xFF;
        lengths[8 + i] = ciphertext_length >> (8 * i) & 0xFF;
    }
    poly_blocks(p, lengths, 0, 1, 1 << 24);
    return p;
}

/// Encrypt and authenticate: RFC 8439 §2.8, `AEAD_CHACHA20_POLY1305`'s
/// encryption direction. `key` (32 octets) and `nonce` (12 octets) at any
/// other length **trap**, per this module's header; `aad` may be empty and
/// any length otherwise. The result is `plaintext`'s length plus 16 --
/// ciphertext, then the tag.
pub bytes seal(bytes key, bytes nonce, bytes plaintext, bytes aad) {
    int length = plaintext.size();
    if (blocks_for(length) > 0xFFFF_FFFF - 1) {
        trap("chacha20: counter must fit in 32 bits");
    }
    Array<int> state = ietf_state(key, nonce);
    bytes out = [0; length + 16];
    chacha20_xor(state, 1, 0, plaintext, length, out);
    Array<int> p = aead_state(state, aad, out, length);
    bytes tag = poly_finish(p);
    for (int i in 0 .. 16) {
        out[length + i] = tag[i];
    }
    return out;
}

/// Verify and decrypt: RFC 8439 §2.8's other direction. `Err(Error.Short)` if
/// `sealed` is not even long enough to hold a tag; `Err(Error.Tag)` if the
/// tag does not match -- in which case nothing about `sealed` should be
/// trusted, `aad` included, and this returns before decrypting anything.
/// `key` and `nonce` follow `seal`'s rule about length.
pub Result<bytes, Error> open(bytes key, bytes nonce, bytes sealed, bytes aad) {
    if (sealed.size() < 16) {
        return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Short);
    }
    int length = sealed.size() - 16;
    Array<int> state = ietf_state(key, nonce);
    Array<int> p = aead_state(state, aad, sealed, length);
    bytes want = poly_finish(p);
    if (!consttime.is_equal(want, sealed.substr(length, sealed.size()))) {
        return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Tag);
    }
    bytes out = [0; length];
    chacha20_xor(state, 1, 0, sealed, length, out);
    return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(out);
}
